Template: sysible/tools
Type: multiselect
Choices: terraform, vault, consul, nomad, packer, boundary, aws-cli
Default:
Description: Optional software to install
 These tools are NOT bundled in the Sysible Server image, so they are
 offered here as a choice. HashiCorp's tools (terraform, vault, consul, nomad,
 packer, boundary) are BUSL-1.1 licensed, not open source; selecting them means
 you accept HashiCorp's licence (https://www.hashicorp.com/bsl). aws-cli is
 installed from Amazon's official channel.
 .
 Anything you select is installed automatically the first time this system boots
 with a working network connection. OpenTofu (the open Terraform drop-in),
 gcloud, the Azure CLI, Docker, kubectl, Helm and Ansible are already included.
 .
 You can add or remove these tools at any time later with:  sudo sysible-tools

Template: sysible/apps
Type: multiselect
# Choices-C carries the ids the rest of the system uses; Choices carries what the
# operator reads. db_get returns the C value, so the full product names below can
# say what the software IS without changing a single thing downstream. cdebconf in
# this build implements Choices-C (libdebconf.so) and d-i's own anna, localechooser
# and console-setup-udeb templates use it. NO COMMAS inside a choice: the list is
# comma-separated and an unescaped comma silently splits one entry into two.
Choices-C: slop, controller, slep, connect
Choices: Sysible Linux Operations Platform (SLOP) - one front door for all three, Sysible Controller - fleet console, Sysible Linux Engineering Platform (SLEP) - infrastructure automation, Sysible Connect - terminal in the browser
Default: slop
Description: Sysible software to set up on first boot
 Tick what this server should run. These are the same four choices that
 `sudo install-sysible` offers after the install, so nothing chosen here is
 final.
 .
 The Sysible Linux Operations Platform (SLOP) puts the Sysible Controller, the
 Sysible Linux Engineering Platform (SLEP) and Sysible Connect behind one front
 door at https://slop.lan/ with a single sign-in. It is the recommended choice
 and it already covers all three, so ticking it alongside the others adds
 nothing -- if it is ticked, it is what gets installed.
 .
 The other three are the same applications on their own addresses: the Sysible
 Controller is the fleet console, the Sysible Linux Engineering Platform (SLEP)
 is infrastructure automation, and Sysible Connect is a terminal in the browser.
 .
 Tick nothing to set nothing up. Any of this can be changed afterwards with:
 sudo install-sysible
 .
 Whatever you pick is installed on the first boot with a working network: the
 source is cloned from GitHub and the containers are built from Docker Hub, so
 it needs both and it is not quick. Nothing listens on the network until it
 completes.
